For years my PC had problems I couldn't explain. Games would stutter out of nowhere, and the desktop would lag for no reason. I had exactly one fix: restart it. Sometimes that worked, and I never asked why.

Today I couldn't restart. My wife was halfway through a movie streaming from the media server on this same machine. So for the first time I had to find out what a restart actually fixes.

It fixes broken apps. Mine had two. NVIDIA's overlay had leaked 12.7 GB of memory. And Razer's mouse software had left 32,709 dead processes behind, which made every frame on my screen wait while the kernel walked a list of programs that no longer existed.

## 30 fps last time, 2 fps today

Subnautica 2 had been smooth the last time I played it. Today it ran at 2 fps. I pointed Claude Code at the problem, and we worked through the suspects. Each guess became a command, and each command either backed it up or ruled it out:

<SuspectList suspects={[
  { name: "A 4K movie transcoding on the same GPU", evidence: "real: Jellyfin was burning subtitles into a 4K HDR stream, decoder at 76%. Fixed. 2 → 30 fps, but still stuttering", guilty: false },
  { name: "Thermals", evidence: "CPU at 94 °C, but still boosting to 3.9 GHz. Not throttling", guilty: false },
  { name: "NVIDIA Overlay leaking memory", evidence: "real: one overlay process from 20 days ago held 12.7 GB, with commit at 48.6 of 49.9 GB. Killed it. Stutter unchanged", guilty: false },
  { name: "Frame pacing, G-Sync", evidence: "turned G-Sync on. Still stuttering, and now I noticed the terminal was too", guilty: false },
  { name: "Something inside the Windows kernel", evidence: "the System process was burning 1.1–1.4 cores nonstop", guilty: true },
]} />

Two of those "not the cause" rows are real bugs that a restart would have quietly wiped away. The terminal stuttering was the real clue. If everything stutters, the problem sits below everything.

## The trace

A 10-second kernel trace with call stacks (xperf, run as admin) showed where the System process was spending its time:

```
dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc              75.81%
  dxgmms2.sys!VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf      75.28%
    ntkrnlmp.exe!NtUpdateWnfStateData          74.68%
      ntkrnlmp.exe!ExpWnfFindScopeInstance     74.30%   (57.9% exclusive)
ntkrnlmp.exe!memcmp                            15.97%   (exclusive)
```

To see why that stalls your whole screen, you need to know a little about how Windows draws.

<StallChain title="how a dead program stalls your screen" steps={[
  { plain: "Every window, your game and your terminal included, renders into GPU memory. The Desktop Window Manager (DWM) composites those surfaces into the frame you see.", kernel: "dwm.exe, dxgkrnl.sys" },
  { plain: "GPU memory is shared, so one part of the kernel manages it for everyone: the video memory manager, VidMm.", kernel: "dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc" },
  { plain: "When memory gets tight (a game using 7 of 8 GB will do it), VidMm evicts allocations and notifies processes so they can shrink.", kernel: "VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf" },
  { plain: "That notification goes through WNF, the kernel's internal pub/sub. Before it can publish, the kernel has to find the right scope instance, and it does that by walking a list and comparing entries one by one.", kernel: "ExpWnfFindScopeInstance + memcmp", stat: "74% of System CPU" },
  { plain: "The list was bloated by processes that had exited but never been cleaned up.", kernel: "33,015 process objects, 306 alive", stat: "32,709 zombies" },
  { plain: "While VidMm's worker is busy walking that list, every app waiting on GPU memory waits too, and that includes DWM. So the whole screen hitches, not just the game.", kernel: "System process", stat: "~1.25 cores, nonstop" },
]} />

## 32,709 processes that don't exist

When a program exits but something still holds a handle to it, Windows can't free it, and it stays around as a zombie. I compared the kernel's process object count with the list of processes actually running:

```
Live processes: 306   Process objects in kernel: 33,015   => zombies ~ 32,709
```

The top handle holders were Razer Game Manager (33,695 handles) and Razer Synapse Service (28,193). Game Manager watches every process you launch, so it can tell when you start a game. It looks like it opens a handle to each one and never closes it. Over the 20 days since my last restart, that's about 1,600 new zombies a day, and every one makes the list longer.

Drag the slider back to zero to see what a restart does:

<LinearScan />

A linear search is fine when the list is short, and fresh after a reboot it is. By day 20 every lookup is about 100 times more expensive, and it happens every time a game pushes GPU memory. That's why the slowdown creeps up over weeks, and why a restart "fixes" it.

I stopped both Razer services. Within a minute the zombies dropped from 32,709 to 260, and the System process fell from about 1.25 cores to 0.2. The stutter disappeared, with no restart and no interrupted movie.

To be clear about what's proven: the stack and the before-and-after numbers are measured. That the zombies are what bloats this particular list is my reading of the evidence. The two dropped together, at the same moment and by about the same factor. But I haven't seen the Windows source.

## Check yours

Open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
(Get-Counter '\Objects\Processes').CounterSamples[0].CookedValue - (Get-Process).Count
```

A few hundred is normal. If you see thousands, something is leaking, and this will usually tell you what:

```powershell
Get-Process | Sort HandleCount -Desc | Select -First 5 Name, HandleCount
```

I'd love to know what other people find. Post your number and your top offender in the comments.

## The restart was hiding things

Restarting is a great fix and a terrible diagnosis. It clears every leak at once, so you never learn which app leaked, and the problem just builds back up. Being unable to restart was the best thing that could have happened to this PC. In one afternoon it turned up a 12.7 GB leak in NVIDIA's overlay, a media server burning subtitles into 4K video on my gaming GPU, and my mouse software quietly slowing every frame on my screen.

My gaming script now disables Razer's services and warns me if the zombie count climbs again. And I keep wondering how many of the restarts I've done over the years to "fix" a stutter were hiding something like this.
